
{"id":"764f439f-bd49-494c-95f6-a85d42fbbad8","object":"chat.completion","created":1784983855,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No root found\\\")\\n    initial_counts = {}\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        initial_counts[sym] = sum(1 for n in nodes if n['symbol'] == sym)\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if initial_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present\\\")\\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    global_counts = dict(initial_counts)\\n    removed_ids = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        symbol = node['symbol']\\n        if symbol in required_symbols and global_counts[symbol] - 1 <= 0:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\\n            global_counts[symbol] -= 1\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if global_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' lost\\\")\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_ids, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that takes a payload with nodes list and required_symbols list, and returns an object with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nThe task: grammar-guided node reduction. The description suggests we need to remove nodes that are removable (removable=true) while preserving required symbols. Also we must consider depth, tie-breaking by node ID.\n\nThe procedure: we have a tree defined by nodes with parent references. Root has parent null. We need to decide which nodes to remove. The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nEssentially: For each removable node, we can consider removing it if its removal doesn't cause the loss of any required symbol. Since removal of a node removes it and possibly its subtree? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests that if we remove a node, its children are also removed? Or we might choose to keep children? But \"Never remove... a node with retained children\" implies we cannot remove a node if its children are retained. So we must first decide which children to retain/remove.\n\nIt's a reduction: we want to minimize the tree while preserving required symbols. Typically, we try to remove nodes unless they are necessary to cover required symbols.\n\nThe problem statement is a bit vague, but we can interpret from the examples: They have nodes with removable flag. The required_symbols list: root and s1. In the first case, nodes with removable=true: n2, n4, n6. Which ones should be removed? n2 is removable, but it has child n4 (removable). If we remove n2, we lose its children. n4 is removable but its removal might be okay if its symbol s1 is already covered elsewhere? There is n1 with symbol s1. So n4 symbol s1 is also covered by n1, so n4 might be removable. n6 is removable with symbol s0, but s0 is not required, so n6 can be removed. However, the problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node if we retain its children. That suggests we need to decide a set of nodes to keep such that all required symbols are present, and we aim to minimize the number of nodes (or maximize removal). The process: deeper nodes first, so we consider removal of n4, n6, then n2? But we must ensure that removals are valid given the decisions.\n\nLet's examine the expected output for the first case? Not given. But we can infer from algorithm description: \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" - Perses is a known technique: it reduces a parse tree by trying to delete subtrees while preserving a property (interestingness). The property here is that required symbols are present. So we need to find a subset of nodes to keep such that the set of symbols in the kept nodes (presumably the symbols of the nodes themselves, not just leaves?) includes all required_symbols. Also, we cannot remove a node if its children are kept (i.e., we can only remove a node if its entire subtree is removed or if we keep none of its children? Actually, \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" means if any child is retained, the parent must be retained. So removal is only allowed for leaf subtrees? But you can remove internal node if you also remove all its descendants.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: given a tree, we want to remove as many nodes as possible (subject to order constraints: deeper first) while ensuring that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node in the retained set with that symbol. And we cannot remove a node unless all its descendants are also removed.\n\nThis is similar to selecting a subset of nodes that form a forest covering required symbols, minimizing the number of retained nodes (or maximizing removal). But we need to produce the removal order and retained set.\n\nAlgorithm: We can perform a post-order traversal (deeper first). For each node, if it is removable and its entire subtree can be removed without losing any required symbol, then we can remove it. But we must ensure that after removal, required symbols are still present in the remaining tree (including ancestors and other branches). So we need to check if the required symbols covered by this node's subtree are also covered elsewhere. If so, we can delete the entire subtree.\n\nSimplify: For each required symbol, we need at least one node with that symbol remaining. So we can compute, for each node, the set of required symbols it uniquely provides (i.e., symbols that appear in its subtree but not in any other retained node). But since we are deciding removal, we can compute from bottom-up: mark nodes that are \"necessary\" to preserve required symbols.\n\nStandard approach: Perform a DFS, compute the set of required symbols present in the subtree. Then, for each node, we can decide to remove it if:\n- It is removable (removable=true).\n- Its removal does not cause any required symbol to be missing from the overall tree (i.e., the required symbols in its subtree are also present in other parts of the tree that are not being removed).\n- And it has no retained children (so we must first process children and decide if they are removed or retained; if any child is retained, parent cannot be removed).\n\nWe need to process deeper nodes first.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n1. Build a dict from node id to node object, and also build children list for each node.\n2. Identify root (parent null).\n3. Perform a post-order traversal (using recursion or stack) to process nodes from leaves upward. For each node, we need to decide if it should be retained. Initial assumption: all nodes are retained. Then we try to remove removable nodes bottom-up if possible.\n\nBetter: We'll maintain a set `retained` initially containing all node ids. Then iterate nodes sorted by depth descending (deepest first), and for each removable node, check if it can be removed. Conditions:\n- Node is not root (but root is not removable anyway).\n- Node is removable.\n- All required symbols that appear in the subtree of this node are also present in other retained nodes (outside this subtree). But careful: because removal of this node also removes all its descendants (since if we remove parent, we must remove children too; the rule \"never remove a node with retained children\" implies that if we decide to remove parent, we must also remove all children). So when we consider removing a node, we effectively remove its entire subtree. So we need to check if removing that subtree would eliminate any required symbol that is not covered elsewhere.\n\nSo for each node, compute the set of required symbols that appear in its subtree (including its own symbol). Then, before removing, check if for every symbol in that set, there exists another retained node (not in this subtree) that also has that symbol. If yes, we can remove the entire subtree.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Precompute for each node the set of required symbols in its subtree (by DFS).\n- Also compute a global count of how many times each required symbol appears in the retained tree initially (all nodes). Since we will remove nodes, we need to update counts as we remove. Better to dynamically check: when considering removal of a node, compute the symbols that would be lost (the subtree's required symbols). Then check if any of those symbols would be reduced to zero after removal. We need to know the current count of each required symbol outside this subtree. That is total count minus the count in this subtree (but careful: if some nodes in subtree have already been removed? Actually we process bottom-up, so when we consider a node, its descendants may have already been removed if they were removable. But we cannot remove a node if its children are retained, so if a child was not removed, we cannot remove the parent. So we should process all nodes, but only remove a node if its entire subtree is already removed? That would be inconsistent: we remove from leaves upward. So we need to decide removal status for children first. If a child is removed, then the parent's subtree counts should not include that child's symbol contributions (since they are already gone). Therefore, we should compute counts dynamically during the post-order traversal.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. Build tree: adjacency children list and parent map.\n2. Compute depth of each node (given already in payload, but we can trust it or recompute).\n3. Initialize retained set = set of all node ids.\n4. Initialize a dict `subtree_symbol_counts` -> for each node, a dict of required symbol->count contributed by its subtree that are currently retained (including itself). We'll update as we remove nodes.\n5. Perform post-order traversal (recursive or stack). For each node (from leaves upward):\n   - Compute `subtree_counts` for that node: start with its own symbol if required, then add children's counts (if child is retained, include its subtree_counts; if child is removed, ignore).\n   - Then, if the node is removable (and not root), check if we can remove it: that would be equivalent to removing all nodes in its subtree (since if we remove node, all descendants must be removed because they have no parent? Actually, if we remove parent, children become orphaned; but the rule says \"never remove a node with retained children\", so we can only remove node if all its children are also removed. So if any child is retained, we cannot remove parent. So we must check that all children are removed (i.e., not in retained set). Then, if all children are removed, removing the node means we remove the entire subtree (node + already removed descendants? But descendants are already removed, so removing the node just removes itself). The condition for removal: All required symbols that are contributed by this subtree (including its own symbol and any retained descendants? But if descendants are retained, they wouldn't be, because children must be removed first) must still be present outside this subtree.\n   - So we need to check that for each required symbol s that would be lost (i.e., the count of s in `subtree_counts`), the total count of s in the entire tree MINUS the count in this subtree is > 0. But total count is the initial total counts (which we can compute once) minus the counts already removed from previously removed nodes. So we need to maintain global remaining counts for each required symbol.\n   - If the condition holds, then we remove the node: add node.id to removed list, remove from retained set, and subtract its `subtree_counts` from global remaining counts.\n   - If not, we keep it (and it remains in retained set, and its subtree counts contribute to parent's counts).\n\nBecause we process bottom-up, by the time we reach a node, its children have already been processed (and possibly removed). So if a node is removable and all its children are either removed (absent from retained) or were never there (leaf), then we can consider removing it. But we also need to ensure that its own symbol (if required) is covered elsewhere. So we check global counts.\n\nEdge cases: required symbols might appear multiple times. We need to preserve at least one occurrence. So we use counts > 0 after subtraction.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Create a dict `node_map`: id -> node object.\n- Build children list for each node.\n- Compute initial total counts for each required symbol (count of nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols). Because required symbols list may have duplicates? Typically unique. But we'll count occurrences.\n\n- We'll use a recursive function `post_order(node_id)` that returns a dict of symbol->count for the subtree (only currently retained nodes). But we need to know which children are retained to compute this. We can do iterative or use stack for postorder.\n\nSimpler: Use a list of nodes sorted by depth descending. Process each node, but we need to know children status. We can maintain a set of removed nodes initially empty. For each node in order of decreasing depth:\n   - If node is removable and (not root):\n        - Check if all its children are either not retained (i.e., removed or non-existent? Actually children that are not in retained set are removed; but we must ensure that children that are retained are also not removable? Wait: if a child is retained, we cannot remove the parent. So child must be removed (i.e., its id not in retained) for parent to be removable. So condition: for each child id, child not in retained set.\n        - Compute the set of required symbols that would be lost from the subtree of this node. But since children are already removed, the only symbol lost is the node's own symbol (and maybe some symbols from removed descendants? No, removed descendants already lost their symbols). So the lost symbols are exactly the symbols of the node itself (if required). Also, if there are descendants that are still retained? They cannot be because children are all removed, so no retained descendants. So the only contribution is the node's own symbol.\n        - However, we also need to consider that if we remove this node, we might also remove opportunity to cover required symbols via ancestors? No, ancestors are separate.\n        - So condition: For symbol of this node (if it's a required symbol), the global count of that symbol minus 1 (for this node) is still > 0. If the node's symbol is not required, then no loss.\n        - Additionally, we need to ensure that the required symbols that were covered by descendants are already covered elsewhere? But those descendants are already removed, so their symbols were already subtracted from global counts. So it's fine.\n        - However, there is a nuance: What if a descendant was retained because its symbol was needed? That descendant would not be removed, so the parent would not be removable because child is retained. So the case where a descendant is retained and we later consider removing parent won't happen because child retained blocks parent removal. So our top-down depth-sorted approach might fail if a child is retained but later becomes removable? But we process deeper first, so children are processed before parents. So by the time we process parent, children have been processed and either removed or retained. So condition: if any child is retained, parent cannot be removed. That aligns.\n\nBut what about a scenario where a node's own symbol is required but its descendant also had that symbol, and the descendant was removed because it was not needed. Then the node's symbol might be the only remaining occurrence. So we should not remove it. Our condition using global counts will catch that: after removing the descendant, global count of that symbol decreased. When we consider the node, global count including the node is, say, 1. Subtracting 1 would be 0, so we cannot remove.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Build initial set of retained = {all node ids}.\n- Initialize global counts for each required symbol: count of nodes with that symbol.\n- Create removed_ids list in order of removal (the order we process).\n- Process nodes sorted by depth descending (largest depth first). For ties in depth, use node id as tie-break (string compare). \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" The instruction says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort by (-depth, node_id). But node_id strings, we can compare lexicographically? It says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we can sort by (-depth, node_id). Since node_id strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc., lexicographic order is fine.\n- For each node in that order:\n    - If node.id not in retained: skip (already removed as part of parent's removal? Actually if parent removed earlier, the child would be removed from retained at that time. Since we process deeper first, child would have been processed before parent. But if we remove parent, we also need to remove children that are still retained. However, our algorithm currently only removes a node if all children are already removed. So if we haven't removed children yet, we can't remove parent. So when we later remove a parent, the children should already be removed from retained. But we need to ensure that when we remove parent, we also remove it from retained and add to removed list. But children are already removed, so we don't need to remove them again. However, the parent removal order should be after children. That's fine.\n    - Check if node is removable and node.id != root_id (though root removable is false anyway).\n    - Check that for every child in children[node.id], child not in retained (i.e., already removed). If any child is retained, skip this node (cannot remove).\n    - Compute the symbol loss: if node.symbol in required_symbols, then the loss = 1; else 0.\n    - For each required symbol that would be lost (only node's own symbol if required), check if global_counts[symbol] - loss > 0. Since we haven't subtracted this node yet.\n    - If all conditions satisfied:\n        - Remove node from retained set.\n        - Append node.id to removed_ids.\n        - Update global_counts: if node.symbol in required_symbols, global_counts[node.symbol] -= 1.\n- After processing all nodes, retained set contains the nodes to keep.\n- Sort retained_ids? The output requires retained_node_ids array, presumably sorted? The task says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we should sort retained_node_ids (probably alphabetically/numerically). But what sort order? Likely ascending string order. We'll sort the list.\n- removed_node_ids: order of removal as we processed (deep to shallow). That is the actual removal order.\n\nNote: The instruction says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" In sorting for processing, we already use that. In output for retained IDs, sorting is separate.\n\nEdge case: What if a node has a child that is removable but we chose to retain it because its symbol is needed? Then child retained, so parent cannot be removed. That's correct.\n\nNow, we need to handle the case where required symbols might be missing entirely? The problem says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if after processing, any required symbol has global count 0 (i.e., not present in any retained node), we should raise ValueError. But we only remove nodes that preserve remaining counts >0, so that should not happen if we start with all nodes and required symbols are present initially. But if some required symbol is not present in any node initially, we should raise ValueError.\n\nAlso check for structural validity: nodes have required fields? Not specified, but we can assume correct.\n\nImplementation in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build map\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n.get('parent')\n        if p is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root found\")\n\n    # Check all required symbols present initially\n    initial_counts = {}\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        initial_counts[sym] = sum(1 for n in nodes if n['symbol'] == sym)\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if initial_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present in any node\")\n\n    # Initialize retained set and global counts\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    global_counts = dict(initial_counts)  # copy\n    removed_ids = []  # order of removal\n\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then node id ascending as tie-break\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue  # already removed\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Check that all children are removed\n        all_children_removed = all(cid not in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if not all_children_removed:\n            continue\n        # Determine symbol loss\n        symbol = node['symbol']\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\n            loss = 1\n            # Check if we can lose this occurrence\n            if global_counts[symbol] - loss <= 0:\n                continue\n        else:\n            loss = 0\n        # Remove node\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\n        if loss:\n            global_counts[symbol] -= 1\n\n    # After processing, check that all required symbols still present\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if global_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{sym}' lost after reduction\")\n\n    # Return result\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)  # sorted ascending string\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n\nNow, we need to test with given fixtures? Not provided expected outputs, but we can reason. For first payload: nodes: root(symbol root, not removable), n1(s1, not removable), n2(s2, removable), n3(s0, not removable), n4(s1, removable, child of n2), n5(s2, not removable, child of n3), n6(s0, removable, child of n1). Required symbols: root, s1.\n\nProcess deeper first: depths: n4 depth2, n5 depth2, n6 depth2 (tie), then n1, n2, n3 depth1, root depth0. Sort by depth desc: maybe n4, n5, n6, then n1,n2,n3.\n\nConsider n6: removable, parent n1 (depth1). Children: none (leaf). Check children removed: none, so true. symbol s0 not required, so no loss. Condition satisfied: remove n6. retained removes n6, global counts unchanged, removed_ids = ['n6'].\n\nn5: not removable => skip.\n\nn4: removable, parent n2, children none. symbol s1 is required. global_counts initially: s1 appears in n1 and n4 -> count=2. Subtract 1 -> 1 >0, so condition holds. Remove n4. global_counts[s1]=1. removed_ids=['n6','n4'].\n\nn2: removable, parent root. children: n4 is already removed (not in retained). So all children removed. symbol s2 not required. => Remove n2. retained remove n2. removed_ids=['n6','n4','n2'].\n\nn1: not removable => skip.\n\nn3: not removable => skip.\n\nroot: not removable.\n\nRemaining retained: root, n1, n3, n5? Also n1 is retained, n3 retained, n5 retained. So retained node ids: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root']? But root's id is \"root\", so sorted strings: 'n1','n3','n5','root' (since 'n' < 'r'). That seems plausible. Required symbols: root (root node symbol) and s1 (n1 retained). So all good.\n\nThis matches typical reduction: removed n6, n4, n2. The other nodes remain.\n\nWe should also handle cases where removable nodes exist deeper but removal might be blocked by children retained. The algorithm covers.\n\nNow produce the implementation as a JSON string with key \"implementation\". Ensure no markdown, just JSON.\n\nThe function must not mutate case, and we read only fields present.\n\nWe'll write the source as a single string. Need to escape backslashes, quotes, newlines. Use json.dumps in Python to generate the JSON string, but we are writing the response in text. We'll manually write JSON.\n\nFor safety, we can write the Python code with proper indentation, then escape for JSON.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n.get('parent')\n        if p is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root found\")\n    initial_counts = {}\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        initial_counts[sym] = sum(1 for n in nodes if n['symbol'] == sym)\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if initial_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present\")\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    global_counts = dict(initial_counts)\n    removed_ids = []\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        symbol = node['symbol']\n        if symbol in required_symbols and global_counts[symbol] - 1 <= 0:\n            continue\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\n            global_counts[symbol] -= 1\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if global_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{sym}' lost\")\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nNow format as JSON string. We'll produce a single line or multiline? Must be valid JSON string with escaped newlines. We'll use triple quotes? No, we need to write the string inside JSON. Better to write the entire response as a JSON object with key \"implementation\". We'll use a tool to generate, but manually:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No root found\\\")\\n    initial_counts = {}\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        initial_counts[sym] = sum(1 for n in nodes if n['symbol'] == sym)\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if initial_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present\\\")\\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    global_counts = dict(initial_counts)\\n    removed_ids = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        symbol = node['symbol']\\n        if symbol in required_symbols and global_counts[symbol] - 1 <= 0:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\\n            global_counts[symbol] -= 1\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if global_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' lost\\\")\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_ids, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids}\"}\n\nThat's the implementation string. We need to ensure all quotes are escaped. In JSON, backslashes escape characters. Newline is \\n. We have f-strings with single quotes inside double quotes, which is fine. The f-string has curly braces for formatting but inside Python code, they are literal; however, when embedding in JSON, we need to escape curly braces? No, curly braces are not special in JSON strings, they are just characters. So fine.\n\nBut we have double quotes inside the Python string that are inside JSON string. They need to be escaped with backslash. For example: case['nodes'] uses single quotes, no issue. But we have f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present\\\" which uses double quotes. In JSON, we need to escape those double quotes: \\\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present\\\". However, the Python source itself contains double quotes inside an f-string. When we put that Python source as a JSON string value, the double quotes must be escaped with backslash. So we need to replace \" with \\\" inside the Python code string.\n\nSimilarly, backslashes in Python code (like \\n) are not used; we have newline as actual newline in string? In our representation, we are using \\n to represent newline in the JSON string. So the Python source code in the JSON string should have actual newlines escaped as \\n. So we need to write a single line with \\n to represent newlines. Alternatively, we can keep the Python code as a multiline literal in the JSON string by using newline characters, but they must be escaped as \\n. It's easier to write as a single line with explicit \\n.\n\nWe need to generate the JSON response. Let's write the Python code as a string with escaped double quotes and newlines.\n\nWe can do programmatically, but here we produce manually.\n\nDefine the Python source as a raw string and then replace.\n\nI'll write the JSON:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No root found\\\")\\n    initial_counts = {}\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        initial_counts[sym] = sum(1 for n in nodes if n['symbol'] == sym)\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if initial_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present\\\")\\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    global_counts = dict(initial_counts)\\n    removed_ids = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        symbol = node['symbol']\\n        if symbol in required_symbols and global_counts[symbol] - 1 <= 0:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_ids.append(nid)\\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\\n            global_counts[symbol] -= 1\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if global_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' lost\\\")\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_ids, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids}\"\n}\n\nCheck: The Python code has double quotes inside f-strings: f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present\\\" and f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' lost\\\". In the JSON string, these double quotes are escaped as \\\". So it's correct.\n\nAlso note that the Python code uses single quotes for dict keys in 'nodes', 'id', etc. That's fine.\n\nNow produce the final answer as JSON without any additional text. Ensure no trailing spaces.\n\nI'll output the JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens"